<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-16"?>
<!--
  sma-daemon-watch-windows.task.xml — the WATCHDOG's own unit: who watches the watcher.

  THE DECLARATION SAYS UTF-16 AND THE FILE ON DISK IS UTF-8, exactly as in its two
  siblings, and for the same measured reason — Task Scheduler takes this document as a
  .NET string, which is UTF-16, so a declaration that says UTF-8 makes MSXML refuse with
  «unable to switch the encoding» at the end of the first line.

  WHY A THIRD TASK. The other two bring the daemon UP — at 23:30, and at logon. Neither
  notices a daemon that DIED at three in the afternoon, and nothing else can: the window
  is the daemon's own web face and the Telegram polling is the same process, so one death
  takes both channels at once and reads as silence. `supervisor/daemon-watch.mjs` is what
  notices, tells the owner and lifts — and started from a terminal it lives exactly as long
  as that terminal does, which moves the dependency instead of removing it. This unit is
  what removes it.

  WHAT IT IS NOT. It is not a second way to start the daemon. The watchdog knocks on the
  door and, on a fall, runs the SAME lift the tasks above run (start-daemon-windows.ps1);
  a machine where the daemon is already answering costs it one HTTP request every fifteen
  seconds and nothing else. Nor does it fight the owner: a daemon stopped on purpose leaves
  no process record behind, and the watchdog reads that difference and stays out of the way.

  ORDER MATTERS, AND IT IS BOUGHT WITH A DELAY. The daemon's own logon task waits 30
  seconds; this one waits two minutes, so the ordinary boot is already serving by the time
  the watchdog looks. Without the gap the watchdog would meet a machine that is merely slow,
  spend its silences on it, and report a fall that never happened.

  RESTARTONFAILURE IS THE LAST TURTLE. A watchdog that dies is a watchdog nobody watches,
  and Task Scheduler is the thing under it that does not. Five attempts a minute apart: a
  watchdog that cannot start five times running has a reason a restart will not fix, and
  saying so in the Task Scheduler history is better than an endless quiet loop.

  SHIPPED DISABLED, like both siblings: <Settings><Enabled>false</Enabled>. Registering AND
  enabling are the machine owner's explicit decision, never a side effect of an install.

  Import (owner only):
    schtasks /Create /TN SMA-Daemon-Watch /XML supervisor\sma-daemon-watch-windows.task.xml
  Replace <SMA_HOME> below with the absolute path of the SMA product clone before import.
-->
<Task version="1.2" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>SMA daemon watchdog — notices a fallen daemon, tells the owner in Telegram and runs the supervisor's own lift. Shipped disabled.</Description>
    <URI>\SMA-Daemon-Watch</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <!-- Two minutes: the daemon's own logon task waits 30 seconds and then has a boot to
           finish. A watchdog that looks earlier is looking at a machine that is only slow. -->
      <Delay>PT2M</Delay>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <!-- The same interactive token as the daemon's own tasks: the watchdog reads the
           owner's daemon config (the door address and the bot token live there) and spawns
           the same lift, so it must stand in the same account. No admin, no service user. -->
      <LogonType>InteractiveToken</LogonType>
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <Enabled>false</Enabled>
    <!-- One watchdog per machine. Two would declare the same fall twice and lift twice. -->
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <StartWhenAvailable>true</StartWhenAvailable>
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <Hidden>false</Hidden>
    <RunOnlyIfIdle>false</RunOnlyIfIdle>
    <WakeToRun>false</WakeToRun>
    <!-- PT0S = no time limit. A watchdog that is killed after 72 hours is not a watchdog. -->
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
      <Count>5</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>
    <Priority>7</Priority>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>node</Command>
      <Arguments>"&lt;SMA_HOME&gt;\supervisor\daemon-watch.mjs"</Arguments>
      <WorkingDirectory>&lt;SMA_HOME&gt;</WorkingDirectory>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
